# Mutation cases for §scxml-5.3 — a `<data>` initializer is typed by the value
# it denotes, so the typed read accessors reach every declaration that has one.
#
# Run with
#   scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/datamodel_type_is_read_from_the_value.cases
#
# Why this file exists rather than only the assertions it guards: the defect it
# is about was a silence. `classify_variables` tested an initializer's first and
# last character for `"`, so `'…'` — the other spelling of the same ECMAScript
# string literal, and the one the AI-loop example uses throughout — declared no
# type and got no accessor. Nothing failed. Every hand-written probe in
# `datamodel_read_accessor.rs` happened to use double quotes, so the suite was
# green over a document with eight unreadable variables. A classifier that
# quietly narrows is exactly the shape a mutation round is for: the question
# below is whether anything would notice it narrowing again.
#
# `sce-build`'s library is compiled into this test binary, so a mutation here
# moves the artifact hash and the round's first check is answerable.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --test datamodel_read_accessor
mutation_targets sce-build/src/analyzer.rs

# ── Strings stop being typed at all ────────────────────────────────
#
# The whole regression, restated at the arm that fixes it. Every string
# declaration falls back to `runtime` — which is the state the AI-loop example
# shipped in, and which no assertion about the numeric half can see.

mutation_case "a string literal declares no type" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/analyzer.rs",
     'Expr::Str(_) => Some("string"),',
     "Expr::Str(_) => None,")
PY

# ── Only the left operand of `+` settles a concatenation ───────────
#
# ECMA-262 3rd ed. §11.6.1 concatenates when EITHER operand is a String. A rule
# that reads only one side types `'Milestone: ' + milestone` and not
# `milestone + ' reached'`, which is the same expression written the other way
# round.

mutation_case "a concatenation is a string only when the string is on the left" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/analyzer.rs",
     'literal_type(right) == Some("string")',
     'literal_type(right) == Some("bool")')
PY

# ── Negation is no longer a number ─────────────────────────────────
#
# `-7` is negation applied to `7`, not a numeric literal, so the arm that
# admits it is separate from the one that admits `7`. Dropping it loses every
# negative budget or offset a document declares.

mutation_case "a negated number declares no type" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/analyzer.rs",
     "op: UnaryOp::Neg | UnaryOp::Pos,",
     "op: UnaryOp::Pos,")
PY

# ── A fractional number claims an integer accessor ─────────────────
#
# The opposite failure to the ones above: typing too much. `read_int` refuses a
# fractional value at run time, so `<data expr="1.5"/>` classified `int` emits
# an accessor that answers `None` for the whole run — a reader that exists and
# never works, which is worse than the honest absence.

mutation_case "a fractional number is offered an integer accessor" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/analyzer.rs",
     "d.fract() == 0.0 && d >= i64::MIN as f64",
     "d >= i64::MIN as f64")
PY

# ── Two arms no longer have to agree ───────────────────────────────
#
# `c ? 'a' : 7` yields whichever arm the condition picks, so it has a type only
# when both arms have the same one. Taking the first arm's answer regardless
# promises a string for an expression that returns a number half the time.

mutation_case "a conditional takes its first arm's type without agreement" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/analyzer.rs",
     "(a == literal_type(b)?).then_some(a)",
     "literal_type(b).map(|_| a)")
PY

# ── A structured literal stops being typed ─────────────────────────
#
# The same silence as the string case, one type later. An array or object
# literal declaring no type takes the accessor away from every structured
# `<data>` — and the one the AI-loop example declares is `screen_rules`, the
# block that decides when a person is NOT woken. A host that cannot read it
# cannot show anyone which decisions are standing, and nothing about the run
# looks different.

mutation_case "an array or object literal declares no type" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/analyzer.rs",
     'Expr::Array(_) | Expr::Object(_) => Some("json"),',
     "Expr::Array(_) | Expr::Object(_) => None,")
PY

# ── Only arrays are structured ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# Half of the arm above. An object literal is as settled as an array literal —
# both construct what they are before anything is evaluated — and a rule that
# admits one is a rule someone stopped writing halfway.

mutation_case "an object literal is not structured" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/analyzer.rs",
     'Expr::Array(_) | Expr::Object(_) => Some("json"),',
     'Expr::Array(_) => Some("json"),')
PY

# ── A name only has to parse, not to be a name ─────────────────────
#
# The json reader is the only one that writes the variable's name into an
# expression, so it is the only one that needs the name to mean that variable
# there. `<data id="screen-rules" expr="[…]"/>` is a legal declaration whose
# reader would evaluate a subtraction — not a refusal, an answer about
# something the document never held — and `screen.rules` a member access.
# Both of those parse. Accepting anything that parses is the weakening that
# looks like a guard and is not one, and it is silent on every document that
# does not use such a name, which is all of them until one does.
#
# The guard is not deleted outright here: dropping the call leaves
# `reachable_as_an_expression` unused, `warnings = deny` refuses the tree, and
# an INCONCLUSIVE round proves nothing about the assertion.

mutation_case "a json name only has to parse as some expression" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/analyzer.rs",
     "Ok(crate::ecmascript::Expr::Ident(parsed)) if parsed == id",
     "Ok(_)")
PY

# ── The identifier guard swallows the scalar readers too ───────────
#
# The opposite over-reach. The scalar readers fetch by key through the
# engine's variable API, where any text does, so applying the json rule to all
# four withdraws accessors that work — a regression dressed as consistency.

mutation_case "the identifier rule is applied to every type" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/analyzer.rs",
     'if var.var_type == "json" && !reachable_as_an_expression(&var.id) {',
     "if !reachable_as_an_expression(&var.id) {")
PY
